-- title:
--   j(q^N) is a root of the modular polynomial over ℚ(j)
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and let `data` be a modular-polynomial packet for $N$, i.e. a bivariate integral polynomial $\Phi \in (\mathbb{Z}[X])[Y]$ together with three properties: $\Phi$ is monic in $Y$; its degree in $Y$ equals `dedekindPsi N` $= \sum_{d \mid N,\ d\ \text{squarefree}} N/d$; and $\Phi$ vanishes when its coefficients, which lie in $\mathbb{Z}[X]$, are mapped into the field of Laurent series $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ by the ring homomorphism `evalAtJ` that evaluates at the $q$-expansion `jq` of the modular invariant $j$, and $Y$ is set equal to `jqN N`, the Laurent series obtained from `jq` by multiplying all exponents by $N$ (substitution $q \mapsto q^N$). Write $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$ for the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by `jq`, and `data.toAdjoin` for the image of $\Phi$ in $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle[Y]$ under coefficientwise application of the homomorphism `evalAtJGen` that sends $X$ to the distinguished generator `jGen` of $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$. The assertion is that evaluating `data.toAdjoin` at `jqN N`, via the inclusion of $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$ into $\mathbb{Q}((q))$, gives $0$.
--
--   This is the statement that $j(q^N)$ is a root of the level-$N$ modular polynomial read as a one-variable polynomial over the field generated by $j(q)$, the first step towards identifying that polynomial with the minimal polynomial of $j(q^N)$ over $\mathbb{Q}(j(q))$ and hence computing the degree $\psi(N)$ of the resulting extension. It is used in the analysis of the modular equation for $X_0(N)$, in particular in the irreducibility statement [`ModularCurve.phiIrreducible_all`](thm.html#ModularCurve.phiIrreducible_all) and in the coefficient computation [`ModularCurve.PhiGen.sum_qTwist_coeff`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PhiGen.sum_qTwist_coeff).
